@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Player scene and input actions") {
        In the next two lessons, we will design the player scene, register custom input
        actions, and code player movement. By the end, you'll have a playable character
        that moves in eight directions.
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating the player scene") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We'll start by creating a `Player` scene from a
            [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d).
            Character bodies are complementary to the area and rigid bodies used in the
            2D game tutorial. Like rigid bodies, they can move and collide with the
            environment, but instead of being controlled by the physics engine,
            **you** dictate their movement. You will see how we use the node's unique
            features when we code the jump and squash mechanics.

            > See also: To learn more about the different physics node types, see the
            > [Physics introduction](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/physics%5Fintroduction.html#doc-physics-introduction).

            We're also going to create a basic rig for our character's 3D model. This
            will allow us to rotate the model later via code while it plays an
            animation.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create a new scene by going to the **Scene** menu in the top-left and
                tapping **New Scene**.

                @Image(source: new_scene.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Tap the **Other Node** button and select the `CharacterBody3D` node
                type to create a `CharacterBody3D` as the root node.

                @Image(source: add_character_body3D.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Rename the
                [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d)
                to `Player`.

                @Image(source: fix-me.png, alt: "The CharacterBody3D node renamed to Player")
            }
            @Step {
                Add a
                [Node3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode3d.html#class-node3d)
                node as a child of `Player`. Select the `Player` node in the **Scene**
                tree and tap the "**+**" button to add a child node. Rename it to
                `Pivot`.

                @Image(source: adding_node3D.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the FileSystem dock, expand the `art/` folder by double-tapping it
                and drag and drop `player.glb` onto `Pivot`.

                @Image(source: instantiating_the_model.webp)
            }
            This should instantiate the model as a child of `Pivot`.

            @Step {
                Rename the newly instantiated `mob` (or `player`) node to `Character`.

                > Note: The `.glb` files contain 3D scene data based on the open
                > source glTF 2.0 specification. They're a modern and powerful
                > alternative to a proprietary format like FBX, which Godot also
                > supports. To produce these files, we designed the model in
                > [Blender 3D](https://www.blender.org/) and exported it to glTF.

                @Image(source: scene_structure.webp)
            }
            As with all kinds of physics nodes, we need a collision shape for our
            character to collide with the environment.

            @Step {
                Select the `Player` node again and add a child node
                [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d).
                In the **Inspector**, on the **Shape** property, add a new
                [SphereShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fsphereshape3d.html#class-sphereshape3d).

                The sphere's wireframe appears below the character.

                @Image(source: add_capsuleshape3d.webp)
            }
            The sphere will be the shape the physics engine uses to collide with the
            environment, so we want it to better fit the 3D model.

            @Step {
                Make the sphere a bit larger by dragging the orange dot in the
                viewport. Mine has a radius of about `0.8` meters.

                @Image(source: sphere_shape.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Then, move the collision shape up so its bottom roughly aligns with
                the grid's plane.

                > Tip: To make moving the shape easier, you can toggle the model's
                > visibility by tapping the **eye icon** next to the `Character` or
                > the `Pivot` nodes.

                @Image(source: moving_the_sphere_up.png)
            }
            @Step {
                Save the scene as `player.tscn`.

                @Image(source: toggling_visibility.webp, alt: "Save the scene as player.tscn")
            }
            With the nodes ready, we can almost get coding. But first, we need to
            define some input actions.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Creating input actions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            To move the character, we will listen to the player's input, like pressing
            the arrow keys. In Godot, while we could write all the key bindings in
            code, there's a powerful system that allows you to assign a label to a
            set of keys and buttons. This simplifies our scripts and makes them more
            readable.

            This system is the Input Map.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                To access the Input Map editor, head to the **Project** menu and
                select **Project Settings...**.

                @Image(source: project_settings.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                At the top of the Project Settings window, there are multiple tabs.
                Tap on **Input Map**. This window allows you to add new actions at
                the top; they are your labels. In the bottom part, you can bind
                keys to these actions.

                Godot projects come with some predefined actions designed for user
                interface design. These will become visible if you enable the **Show
                Built-in Actions** toggle. We could use these here, but instead we're
                defining our own to support gamepads. Leave **Show Built-in Actions**
                disabled.

                @Image(source: input_map_tab.webp)
            }
            We're going to name our actions `move_left`, `move_right`,
            `move_forward`, `move_back`, and `jump`.

            @Step {
                To add an action, write its name in the bar at the top and press
                Enter or tap the **Add** button.

                @Image(source: adding_action.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Create the following five actions: `move_left`, `move_right`,
                `move_forward`, `move_back`, and `jump`.

                @Image(source: actions_list_empty.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                To bind a key or button to an action, tap the "**+**" button to its
                right. Do this for `move_left`. Press the left arrow key and tap
                **OK**.

                @Image(source: left_inputmap.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Bind also the A key, onto the action `move_left`.

                @Image(source: keyboard_keys.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Let's now add support for a gamepad's left joystick. Tap the "**+**"
                button again but this time, select the input within the input tree
                yourself. Select the negative X axis of the left joystick under
                **Joypad Axes**.

                Leave the other values as default and press **OK**.

                > Note: If you want controllers to have different input actions,
                > you should use the Devices option in Additional Options. Device 0
                > corresponds to the first plugged gamepad, Device 1 corresponds to
                > the second plugged gamepad, and so on.

                @Image(source: joystick_axis_input.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Do the same for the other input actions. For example, bind the right
                arrow, D, and the left joystick's positive axis to `move_right`.
                After binding all keys, your interface should look like this.

                @Image(source: move_inputs_mapped.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                The final action to set up is the `jump` action. Bind the Space key
                and the gamepad's A button located under **Joypad Buttons**.

                Your jump input action should look like this.

                @Image(source: jump_input_action.webp)
            }
            That's all the actions we need for this game. You can use this menu to
            label any groups of keys and buttons in your projects.

            In the next part, we'll code and test the player's movement.
        }
    }
}
